course: dictionary
lesson: 9943
title: Tarnish and tarnished
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To tarnish is when metal loses its shine and goes dull or dark. You can see it on silver, brass and copper. «Silver tarnishes because it reacts with sulphur gases in the air and makes a thin black layer.» The words «reacts» and «thin black layer» are your clues: something in the air changes the surface of the metal.
explain: Context can tell you what causes tarnish. «Eggs, onions and some rubber give off sulphur gases, so silver egg spoons tarnish very quickly.» The clue is sulphur gases. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap in your meaning: «silver egg spoons lose their shine very quickly» still makes sense, so you have got it.
explain: Tarnish also has a second meaning, used about people. A «tarnished reputation» means people trust someone less after something bad has happened. The word is not really about metal here. It means that someone's good name has lost its shine.
fact: Tarnish can be a verb (to tarnish) or a noun (the tarnish, the dull layer on the metal).
fact: «Tarnish is a chemical change on the very surface of a metal.»
fact: «Gold almost never reacts with oxygen or sulphur, so it does not tarnish.»
fact: «Tarnished» means lost its shine and gone dull or dark.
fact: A «tarnished reputation» means people think less well of someone; it is a figure of speech, not about real metal.
exercise: «Silver tarnishes because it reacts with sulphur gases in the air.» What does tarnish mean? | *Loses its shine and goes dull or dark | Turns into gold | Is polished until it glows | Gets bigger and heavier
exercise: «Gold almost never reacts with oxygen or sulphur, so it does not tarnish.» Which metal does not tarnish? | Brass | Silver | Copper | *Gold
exercise: Tom says a «tarnished reputation» is about shiny cups. What does it really mean? | People are proud of someone | A cup has been cleaned | *People trust someone less after something bad | Someone has won a prize
test: Why do silver egg spoons tarnish quickly? | Because they are made of gold | *Because eggs, onions and some rubber give off sulphur gases | Because they are kept in the sun | Because they are polished every day
test: In «Tarnish is a chemical change on the very surface of a metal», what is tarnish? | *A chemical change on the surface of a metal | A kind of gold | A tool for polishing | A type of rain
exam: Which sentence uses tarnish as a verb? | *Sulphur in the air will tarnish the silver spoon | Tarnish is a chemical change on metal | A tarnish on his name | The tarnish on the coin was thick
